Multicollinearity in Linear Regression (Part 2)

This topic is one of the assumptions of Multiple Linear Regression. It explains why having highly related input features can make the model unreliable.

What is Multicollinearity?

Multicollinearity occurs when two or more independent variables (features) are highly correlated with each other.

Instead of each feature providing unique information, they contain similar or duplicate information.

Example

Suppose we want to predict a person's salary.

Experience	Age	Salary
2	24	30K
5	27	50K
8	30	70K

Here:

Experience increases with Age.
Age and Experience are highly correlated.

The model becomes confused because both features explain almost the same thing.

Real-Life Analogy

Imagine you have two friends answering the same question in an interview.

Friend 1 gives the answer.
Friend 2 repeats exactly the same answer.

You don't gain any new information.

Similarly, in Machine Learning, highly correlated features provide nearly identical information.

Example

Suppose your dataset has:

Area	Length	Width	Price
1200	40	30	50L
1500	50	30	65L

Notice:

Area = Length × Width

Here,

Area
Length
Width

are strongly related.

The model cannot determine which feature is actually influencing the price.

Why is Multicollinearity a Problem?
1. Unstable Coefficients

Small changes in the data can produce large changes in the learned coefficients.

Example:

Dataset 1

Area coefficient = 5.2
Bedrooms coefficient = 2.3

Dataset 2

Area coefficient = 8.9
Bedrooms coefficient = -1.5

Even though the prediction accuracy may remain similar, the coefficients become unreliable.

2. Difficult Interpretation

When features are highly correlated, we cannot confidently say which feature is responsible for the prediction.

3. Large Standard Errors

The variance of coefficient estimates increases, making them less precise.

4. Feature Importance Becomes Misleading

The model may assign:

High importance to one feature
Very low importance to another

even though both contain almost the same information.

Does Multicollinearity Affect Prediction?
Prediction

Usually No (if new data has the same relationships between features).

Interpretation

Yes, significantly.

This is why multicollinearity is mainly a concern when you want to understand feature effects, not just make predictions.

