How to Remove Multicollinearity?
Method 1: Remove One Feature

If two features provide nearly the same information, remove one.

Example:

Age
Experience

Keep only one if they are highly correlated.

Method 2: Feature Selection

Select only the most useful features using statistical or model-based methods.

Method 3: Principal Component Analysis (PCA)

Transform correlated features into a smaller set of uncorrelated components.

Method 4: Ridge Regression

Ridge Regression reduces the impact of multicollinearity by shrinking coefficients.

This is one of the biggest reasons Ridge Regression is used.

Advantages of Removing Multicollinearity
Stable coefficients
Better interpretability
More reliable feature importance
Reduced variance
Better statistical significance
Interview Questions
1. What is multicollinearity?

It occurs when two or more independent variables are highly correlated with each other.

2. Does multicollinearity affect prediction accuracy?

Usually not much, but it makes the learned coefficients unstable and hard to interpret.

3. How do you detect multicollinearity?
Correlation Matrix
Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)
4. What is a good VIF value?
VIF = 1: No multicollinearity
VIF < 5: Generally acceptable
VIF > 10: Serious multicollinearity
5. How can you solve multicollinearity?
Remove correlated features
Feature selection
PCA
Ridge Regression
